00:03:55,660 --> 00:04:00,320
CONVERSATION 2
You are heading for a completely different world, now that you are about to graduate from high school.

39
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:09,840
I know it's the end of high school, but many of classmates are going on to the same university, and we are still required to study hard.

40
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,960
So what's the difference?

41
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:15,700
Many aspects are different here at the university.

42
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:22,740
The most important one is that you have to take more individual responsibility for your actions.

43
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:28,400
It's up to your own self-discipline—how much efforts you put into study.

44
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:36,100
Living in college dormitories, there are no parents to tell you that study harder or stop wasting time.

45
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:43,440
Lectures have hundreds of students and they are not going to follow you up or question you if you miss the lectures.

46
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:45,600
Nobody cares you mean?

47
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:53,600
It's not that nobody concerned about you, it's just that suddenly at the university you are expected to behave like an adult.

48
00:04:53,600 --> 00:05:02,380
That means concentrating on the direction of your life in general and your own academic performance specifically.

49
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:03,980
For example…?

50
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:11,640
Well, like you need to manage daily, weekly and monthly schedules, so that you study regularly.

51
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:19,780
Be sure to attend all classes and leave enough time to finish your assignments and prepare well for examinations.

52
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:21,480
Ok, and what else is different?

53
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:28,840
Well, in college there are lots of distractions, and you need to control yourself.

54
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:36,100
You will make interesting friends, but you need only keep the friends who respect your students' commitments.

55
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:47,300
Also, there are a lot of wonderful clubs, but you shouldn't allocate too much time to club activities, unless they are directly related to your study.

56
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:56,800
It's also your choice if you want to go out at night, but you will be foolish to let that affect class performance during the day.

57
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:11,760
Well, I'm determined to do well at the university and I guess I'm going to have to grow up fast.

58
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:31,380
Q5. What does the man say about college students as compared with high schoolers?

59
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:50,280
Q6. What are college students expected to do according to the man?

60
00:06:50,280 --> 00:07:09,740
Q7. What kind of friends does the man suggest the woman make as a college student?

61
00:07:09,740 --> 00:08:01,020
Q8. What kind of club activities should college students engage in according to the man?

62
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:09,720
PASSAGE 1
Most successful people are unorthodox persons whose minds wonder outside traditional ways of thinking.

63
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:15,180
Instead of trying to refine old formulas, they invent new ones.

64
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:27,580
When Jean-Claude Killy made the French national ski team in the early 1960s, he was prepared to work harder than anyone else to be the best.

65
00:08:27,580 --> 00:08:36,160
At the crack of dawn, he would run up the slopes with his skis on, an unbelievably backbreaking activity.

66
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:39,180
In the evening, he would do weightlifting and running.

67
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:45,760
But the other team members were working as hard and long as he was.

68
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:52,600
He realized instinctively that simply training harder would never be enough.

69
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:58,340
Killy then began challenging the basic theories of racing technique.

70
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:05,480
Each week, he would try something different to see if he could find a better, faster way down the mountain.

71
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:14,900
His experiments resulted in a new style that was almost exactly opposite the accepted technique of the time.

72
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:23,500
It involved skiing with his legs apart for better balance and sitting back on the skis when he came to a turn.

73
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:29,700
He also used ski poles in an unorthodox way--to propel himself as he skied.

74
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:36,460
The explosive new style helped cut Killy's racing time dramatically.

75
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:44,300
In 1966 and 1967, he captured virtually every major skiing trophy.

76
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:54,200
The next year, he won three gold medals in the Winter Olympics, a record in ski racing that has never been topped.

77
00:09:54,200 --> 00:10:14,340
Killy learned an important secret shared by many creative people: innovations don't require genius, just a willingness to question the way things have always been done.

78
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:33,100
Q9. What does the speaker say about most successful people?

79
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:49,940
Q10. What does the speaker say about Killy's experiments?

80
00:10:49,940 --> 00:11:09,920
Q11. What is said to be Killy's biggest honor in his skiing career?

81
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:18,140
PASSAGE 2
Scientific experiments have demonstrated incredible ways to kill a guinea pig, a small furry animal.

82
00:11:18,140 --> 00:11:23,960
Emotional upsets generate powerful and deadly toxic substances.

83
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:35,200
Blood samples taken from persons experiencing intense fear or anger when injected into guinea pigs have killed them in less than two minutes.

84
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:40,240
Imagine what these poisonous substances can do to your own body.

85
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:46,540
Every thought that you have affects your body chemistry within a split second.

86
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:54,720
Remember how you feel when you are speeding down the highway and a big truck suddenly brakes twenty meters in front of you.

87
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:58,100
A shock wave shoots through your whole system.

88
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:02,360
Your mind produces instant reactions in your body.

89
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:14,180
The toxic substances that fear, anger, frustration and stress produce not only kill guinea pigs but kill us off in a similar manner.

90
00:12:14,180 --> 00:12:21,540
It is impossible to be fearful, anxious, irritated and healthy at the same time.

91
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:25,000
It is not just difficult, it is impossible.

92
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:30,560
Simply put, your body's health is a reflection of your mental health.

93
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:38,920
Sickness will often then be a result of unresolved inner conflicts which in time show up in the body.

94
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:45,340
It is also fascinating how our subconscious mind shapes our health.

95
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:50,360
Do you recall falling sick on a day when you didn't want to go to school?

96
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:53,000
Headaches brought on by fear?

97
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:04,200
The mind-body connection is such that if, for example, we want to avoid something, very often our subconscious mind will arrange it.

98
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:18,760
Once we recognize that these things happen to us, we are half way to doing something about them.

99
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:38,280
Q12. What happens to guinea pigs when blood samples of angry people are injected into them?

100
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:56,680
Q13. What does the speaker say about every thought you have?

101
00:13:56,680 --> 00:14:13,980
Q14. What does the speaker say is impossible?

102
00:14:13,980 --> 00:15:02,700
Q15. What does the passage say about our mind and body?

103
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:17,520
RECORDING 1
Teachers and students alike have experienced the curious paradox that beginners, as a rule, tend to think too little about what they are doing because they think too much about what they are doing.

104
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:21,960
Take for example people who are learning to play basketball or the piano.

105
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:40,080
They have to give so much thought and attention to the low-level mechanics of handling the ball or fingering the keys or reading the music, that they are unable to give any thought to the thing that matters – the game, or the music, respectively.

106
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:43,760
With experts, it's just the other way around.

107
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:56,180
They are open to the tactical possibilities and the musical challenges precisely because they are freed, through skill, from the need to pay attention to the low-level details of how to play.

108
00:15:56,180 --> 00:16:03,660
Indeed, when the expert pays attention to the mechanics, this is liable to disrupt performance.

109
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:12,040
This has led some to say that the expert operates in a zone 'beyond thought', in a state of flow.

110
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:14,360
But this is misleading.

111
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:17,060
Expert performance is not beyond thought.

112
00:16:17,060 --> 00:16:28,900
Smart basketball players or skilled musicians need to pay close attention to the demands of high performance, to the challenges to be overcome.

113
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:39,320
What they don't need to do – what would be a distraction – is to have to think about where their fingers are, or how to control the ball while running.

114
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:45,620
It's not mechanics, but the play itself, that absorbs the experts' intelligence.

115
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:53,820
A nice video published online last month sheds light on expertise and the conscious mind.

116
00:16:53,820 --> 00:16:58,900
The video reports a new study using an eye-tracking device.

117
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:15,300
It turns out that the less-skilled pianist spends more time looking at her fingers than does the expert who, in contrast, is more likely to be looking at the sheet music, or looking ahead at keys he's not yet playing.

118
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:20,700
In general, the expert's gaze was calmer and more stable.

119
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:23,180
This is not a surprising finding.

120
00:17:23,180 --> 00:17:28,440
It supports what we might almost think of as conventional wisdom.

121
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:32,440
But it's remarkable for all that, nonetheless.

122
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:39,200
The eye tracker gives expert and learning performers a glimpse into what they do without thinking about it.

123
00:17:39,200 --> 00:18:02,041
The topic of the nature of skill – and the differences between beginners and experts – has been one of considerable discussion in cognitive science and philosophy.

124
00:18:02,041 --> 00:18:19,400
Q16. What does the speaker say about beginners and expert pianists?

125
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:37,840
Q17. What do smart basketball players do according to the speaker?

126
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:58,680
Q18. What do we learn about the new study published in an online video?

127
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:08,160
RECORDING 2
Every summer when I top up my selection of summer outfits from the department stores, my eyes would nearly pop out of my head.

128
00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:13,780
I'm overwhelmed with the wide range of different slimming products each year.

129
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:20,600
And more shockingly, these products are often advocated by very slim models.

130
00:19:20,600 --> 00:19:27,980
Having lived in Asia for almost ten years now, I've seen various dieting tips come and go.

131
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:37,960
I remember in Japan people heading directly to the food section in the supermarket when the banana diet was at its peak.

132
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:47,720
Then, there was the black tea and oolong tea diet followed by the soybean diet and the tomato juice diet.

133
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:50,760
The list goes on and on.

134
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:56,740
Apart from what people eat, I've also seen many interesting slimming products.

135
00:19:56,740 --> 00:20:08,700
In Hong Kong, I've seen girls wrapping their whole body or both legs up with a special type of slimming tape which is supposed to help make them thinner.

136
00:20:08,700 --> 00:20:16,560
But it just reminded me of the roasted ham my mother usually puts on the dinner table of Christmas.

137
00:20:16,560 --> 00:20:26,500
Then there were the face slimming rollers that were said to improve your blood circulation and make your face smaller.

138
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:30,880
Personally, I do not believe in any of these slimming gadgets.

139
00:20:30,880 --> 00:20:39,260
And I think I have a very different perspective when it comes to the definition of what is beautiful.

140
00:20:39,260 --> 00:20:53,500
Asian women prefer to avoid the sun because being pale or white is considered beautiful, whereas a tanned complexion is considered much more beautiful and sexy in the west.

141
00:20:53,500 --> 00:21:01,080
It is most certainly shaped by a person's culture as well as how they were raised in their childhood.

142
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:05,680
As each summer season approaches, there's no escape from it.

143
00:21:05,680 --> 00:21:11,640
But it's not only women who are affected by this pressure to look good.

144
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:22,720
Men aspire to be able to show off their six packs or their V-shape backs and there's a growing market of slimming pills aimed at men too.

145
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:35,180
I think no matter what diets we follow or what slimming products we obsess ourselves with, at the end of the day there's no magic trick to shape up for the summer.

146
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:40,760
Eat in a balance way and incorporate the right level of physical activity.

147
00:21:40,760 --> 00:21:53,220
For me, this still seems to be the best plan.

148
00:21:53,220 --> 00:22:13,040
Q19. What overwhelms the speaker when she buys her summer outfits each year?

149
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:32,720
Q20. What does the speaker think of girls wrapping their legs up with slimming tape?

150
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:52,240
Q21. What does the speaker think affects people's interpretation of beauty?

151
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:59,320
RECORDING 3
Skin may seem like a superficial human attribute, but it is the first thing we notice about anyone we meet.

152
00:22:59,320 --> 00:23:14,680
As a zoologist focusing on the studies of apes and monkeys, I've been studying why humans evolved to become the naked ape, and why skin comes in so many different shades around the world.

153
00:23:14,680 --> 00:23:24,400
We can make a very good estimate from the fossil record that humans probably evolved naked skin around a million and a half years ago.

154
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:29,120
And meanwhile, they mostly lost their coat of fur.

155
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:34,840
Today, we have a few patches of hair remaining on various parts of our bodies.

156
00:23:34,840 --> 00:23:39,540
But compared with apes and monkeys, we have very little.

157
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:47,740
Basically, we turned our skin darker to serve as a natural sun-protector in the place of the hair we lost.

158
00:23:47,740 --> 00:23:55,460
We think we lost this hair because of the need to keep ourselves cool, when we were moving around vigorously in a hot environment.

159
00:23:55,460 --> 00:24:01,240
We can't really lose heat by breathing quickly and loudly like dogs.

160
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:03,660
We have to do it by sweating.

161
00:24:03,660 --> 00:24:10,040
So we evolved the ability to sweat plentifully, and lost most of our fur.

162
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:14,380
Most animals protect themselves from the sun with fur.

163
00:24:14,380 --> 00:24:21,640
What we did in our ancestry was to produce more permanent natural coloring in our skin cells.

164
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:31,360
This was really an important revolution in human history, because it allowed us to continue to evolve in equatorial environments.

165
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:38,760
It really made it possible for us to continue along the path toward modern humans in Africa.

166
00:24:38,760 --> 00:24:43,560
For most of the human history, we all had dark skin.

167
00:24:43,560 --> 00:24:57,280
What we see today is the product of evolutionary events, resulting from the dispersal of a few human populations out of Africa around 60,000 to 70,000 years ago.

168
00:24:57,280 --> 00:25:14,600
Our species originated around 200,000 years ago, and underwent tremendous diversification, culturally, technologically, linguistically, artistically, for 130,000 years.

169
00:25:14,600 --> 00:25:21,940
After that, a few small populations left Africa to populate the rest of the world.

170
00:25:21,940 --> 00:25:33,580
These early ancestors of modern Eurasians disperse into parts of the world that had more seasonal sunshine and much lower levels of sun radiation.

171
00:25:33,580 --> 00:25:41,540
It's in these populations that we begin to see real changes in the genetic makeup of natural coloring.

172
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:51,560
Today, skin color is evolving via new mixtures of people coming together and having children with new mixtures of skin color genes.

173
00:25:51,560 --> 00:25:56,140
We can see this in almost every large city worldwide.

174
00:25:56,140 --> 00:26:12,021
Not only the coloring genes, but lots of other genes are getting mixed up, too.

175
00:26:12,021 --> 00:26:28,960
Q22. What does the speaker mainly talk about?

176
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:49,980
Q23. What had probably caused humans to lose most of their hair one and a half million years ago?

177
00:26:49,980 --> 00:27:10,940
Q24. What does the speaker say protected early humans from the sun?

178
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:32,193
Q25. What happened when early humans left Africa to populate the rest of the world?
